@page "/help"
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = AppRoles.Viewer)]
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<PageTitle>@(tr ? "Yardım" : "Help") – SqlHarbor</PageTitle>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-lg-3 d-none d-lg-block">
        <nav class="help-toc card"><div class="card-body">
            <div class="section-title mb-2">@(tr ? "İçindekiler" : "Contents")</div>
            @foreach (var (id, title) in tr ? TocTr : Toc)
            {
                <a href="/help#@id">@title</a>
            }
        </div></nav>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-9 help-content">
        <div class="d-flex align-items-center mb-3">
            <h1 class="h4 mb-0 me-auto">@(tr ? "Yardım" : "Help")</h1>
            <div class="btn-group btn-group-sm" role="group" aria-label="Language">
                <button type="button" class="btn @(tr ? "btn-outline-secondary" : "btn-secondary")" @onclick='() => SetLanguageAsync("en")'>English</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn @(tr ? "btn-secondary" : "btn-outline-secondary")" @onclick='() => SetLanguageAsync("tr")'>Türkçe</button>
            </div>
        </div>
        <HelpTabs Active="help" Tr="tr" />

        @if (tr)
        {
            <HelpTr />
        }
        else
        {

        <section id="overview">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p><b>SqlHarbor</b> (technical name SqlDataCollector: service, folders and database objects) collects data on a schedule from several SQL Server <b>sources</b> into tables on a <b>target</b> SQL Server,
               keeps it there for a configurable <b>retention</b> period, and exports it as <b>CSV or Parquet files to object storage</b> – Google Cloud Storage, Amazon S3 (or an S3-compatible store) or Azure Blob Storage.
               Scheduling and execution are handled by <b>Hangfire</b>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Extract jobs</b> copy a table, a view, a query or a stored procedure result from a source database into a target table.</li>
                <li><b>Export jobs</b> write the collected data as CSV or Parquet files (optionally gzip, split into parts) and upload them to a <b>destination</b> (a bucket or container).</li>
                <li><b>Retention</b> removes data older than N days from target tables (instantly, if the table is partitioned by day).</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="quick-start">
            <h2>Quick start</h2>
            <ol>
                <li><b>First sign-in</b> (after the installation, on the server itself): <i>Local administrator</i> asks for a new password – there is no default password.
                    The form is only offered on <code>https://localhost</code>, so nobody on the network can set it first.</li>
                <li><b>Settings</b>: import your HTTPS certificate (.pfx/.p12 or from the Windows store) instead of the self-signed one; upload the Google Cloud Storage credential (Workload Identity Federation configuration or service account key) if you export to GCS.</li>
                <li><b>Destinations</b> (Admin): add the bucket or container the files go to (GCS, S3 / S3-compatible or Azure Blob) and click <i>Test</i>.</li>
                <li><b>Servers</b> (Admin): add your source and target SQL Servers and click <i>Test</i>. Connections use the application's service account.</li>
                <li><b>Permissions</b> (Admin): give AD groups a role, and tell which servers/databases Editors may use as source or target.</li>
                <li><b>Extract Jobs → New job</b>: pick the source server, database and table(s) → <i>Preview</i> → pick the target → <i>Check target</i> → choose the load mode and retention → choose a schedule → <i>Save</i>.</li>
                <li><b>Exports → New export</b>: choose the target database, mode <i>All collected tables</i>, the destination and a nightly schedule (e.g. 01:00).</li>
                <li>Watch <b>Status</b> and <b>Run History</b>. Use <i>Run now</i> on a job page to test it immediately.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="architecture">
            <h2>Architecture: Web and Worker</h2>
            <p>The same application is installed on one or more machines; the setting <code>Collector:Roles</code> decides what each one does.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Role</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Web</b></td><td>This user interface. Saves job definitions and schedules. Never moves data.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Worker</b></td><td>Hangfire Server (Windows Service). Executes extract, export, retention and sync jobs.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p><b>No code is sent to Hangfire.</b> When you click <i>Save</i>:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>The job definition is stored in the metadata database (<code>etl.ExtractJob</code> / <code>etl.ExportJob</code>).</li>
                <li>A recurring entry such as <code>extract-5 → "0 2 * * *" → ExtractRunner.RunAsync(5)</code> is written to the Hangfire database.</li>
                <li>At 02:00 a Worker reads that entry, runs its own copy of <code>ExtractRunner</code>, which reads job 5 from the metadata database and moves the data.</li>
            </ol>
            <h3>Routing work with queues</h3>
            <p>Extract jobs run in the <code>sdc_extract</code> queue, exports in <code>sdc_export</code>, system jobs in <code>sdc_system</code>.
               With <code>Collector:Queues</code> you can decide which Worker takes what. Example: install a Worker on the <b>target SQL Server host</b>
               with <code>Queues = sdc_export</code>. Exports then read the data locally, write the CSV files to that machine's disk and upload them from there,
               while the central Worker runs <code>Queues = sdc_extract,sdc_system</code>.</p>
            <h3>Deployment modes</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Embedded:</b> one app server runs <code>Roles = Web,Worker</code>; the Hangfire tables live in the metadata database.</li>
                <li><b>Shared Hangfire:</b> the UI runs on the app server (<code>Roles = Web</code>) and jobs are stored in the organization's central Hangfire database
                    (own schema <code>hangfire_collector</code>). They are executed either by a SqlDataCollector Worker service on the Hangfire server, or by the
                    organization's existing Hangfire Server host that references <code>SqlDataCollector.Core</code> and listens to the <code>sdc_*</code> queues.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The <b>Status</b> page lists the active Workers and their queues. If no Worker is listed, jobs stay in the queue.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="extract">
            <h2>Extract jobs</h2>
            <p>An extract job reads from a source database and writes into a target table. Rows are streamed (not held in memory) with SqlBulkCopy.
               Every target table gets four technical columns:</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white mono">
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>_LoadId</td><td>Id of the load run (etl.LoadRun). Increases with every run.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>_LoadedAt</td><td>UTC time of the load. Used by retention and partitioning.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>_ExtractJobId</td><td>Job that wrote the row (several jobs may write to one table).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>_SourceServer</td><td>Name of the source server.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>If a run fails, the rows it already wrote are removed again. If a Worker crashes in the middle of a load, the next sync job cleans it up.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="extract-source">
            <h2>Choosing the source</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Table / view:</b> select one or more tables. With several tables, one job per table is created (all columns, same table name on the target).
                    With a single table you can pick columns, add a filter (<i>without</i> the <code>WHERE</code> keyword) and use <code>NOLOCK</code>.</li>
                <li><b>Advanced column list:</b> free SQL expressions, e.g. <code>Id, CAST(Geo.ToString() AS nvarchar(max)) AS Geo</code>. Every column needs a name.</li>
                <li><b>SQL query / procedure:</b> any statement that returns a result set: CTEs, temp tables, <code>EXEC dbo.usp_Report</code>.</li>
                <li><b>Preview</b> runs the source and shows the first 50 rows and the column types.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Only servers and databases you are permitted to use are listed.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="parallel-reads">
            <h2>Parallel reads</h2>
            <p>A large table can be read over several connections at once: <b>Parallel readers</b> in section <i>1 · Source</i> (2, 4, 8 or 16; tables and views only).</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>How:</b> the job measures MIN and MAX of the <b>split column</b> (with the job's filter and watermark), divides the range into equal slices and reads
                    each slice over its own connection (<code>WHERE Id &gt;= @@SliceFrom AND Id &lt; @@SliceTo</code>, parameters of the column's real type). The first slice also
                    reads NULLs and everything below MIN, the last one everything above MAX, so rows added meanwhile are not lost.</li>
                <li><b>Split column:</b> empty = the leading column of the clustered index (else of the primary key). Integer and date/time columns are allowed
                    (tinyint, smallint, int, bigint, date, smalldatetime, datetime, datetime2). Choose an indexed column: each reader then reads only its part of the index.
                    A heap or a view needs an explicit split column.</li>
                <li><b>One stream into the target:</b> the readers hand their rows to one bulk copy, so everything else stays as it was: one load (<code>_LoadId</code>),
                    the integrity count, the watermark, Merge staging and the roll-back of a failed run. If one reader fails, the others are stopped and the rows already
                    written are removed.</li>
                <li><b>When it helps:</b> when reading the source is the slow part – a remote or busy source, wide rows, a filter or watermark that scans. Start with 4;
                    every reader is one more query on the source. The target is still written by one stream; for faster writes use <a href="/help#storage">Columnstore</a>.</li>
                <li><b>Uneven values:</b> slices are equal by value, not by row count. A column with big gaps (e.g. most rows in the last month) gives uneven slices –
                    choose another column or fewer readers.</li>
                <li><b>Limits:</b> not combined with <i>Read a consistent snapshot</i> (each reader would see its own point in time). An empty table and a range with fewer
                    values than readers are read with one reader; query/procedure sources always use one reader. With several tables selected, each job splits on its own
                    clustered index or primary key.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="parallel-writes">
            <h2>Parallel writes</h2>
            <p><b>Parallel writers</b> in section <i>3 · Load</i> (2, 4 or 8) write the rows into the target over several connections at once.
               The rows come from one reader (or from the <a href="/help#parallel-reads">parallel readers</a>) and are dealt out in batches of 1,000 to the writers.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>When it helps:</b> when writing the target is the slow part – usually large loads into <a href="/help#storage">Columnstore</a> tables, where every
                    writer compresses its own rowgroups on its own CPU core. Rowstore tables gain less: all writers insert at the end of the same index.</li>
                <li><b>Still one load:</b> all rows carry the same <code>_LoadId</code>; the integrity count, the watermark, retention and the clean-up of a failed run work
                    as before. If one writer fails, the others are stopped and every row of the run is removed again.</li>
                <li><b>Works with</b> Append, Replace (also with rebuild – the writers load the new table without a table lock), Incremental, daily partitioning and every
                    source type (query and procedure sources too).</li>
                <li><b>Not for Merge:</b> a merge stages its rows in a temporary table of one session, so it always uses one writer.</li>
                <li><b>Columnstore and small loads:</b> each writer fills its own rowgroups; with few rows per writer (below 102,400) they land in the delta store first
                    and SQL Server compresses them later. Use parallel writers for loads of millions of rows.</li>
                <li>Every writer is one more connection to the target and one more CPU core busy on it: start with 4.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="extract-target">
            <h2>Target table and schema policy</h2>
            <p><b>Target schema</b>: for a new job it is filled from the source database name (<code>ERP</code> → <code>erp</code>, <code>Sales DB</code> → <code>sales_db</code>)
               until you type your own. One schema per source avoids collisions between tables with the same name in different sources
               (<code>ERP.dbo.Customers</code> and <code>CRM.dbo.Customers</code>), keeps export folders readable and lets you grant and export per source.
               <code>dbo</code> works too; collector tables only ever touch tables with <code>_LoadId</code>.</p>
            <p><b>Check target</b> compares the source columns with the target table before you save:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><i>Table does not exist</i> → it is created on the first run (with technical columns and indexes).</li>
                <li><i>Matches exactly</i> → data is written.</li>
                <li><i>Differences</i> → listed column by column (missing in target, only in target, too narrow, type mismatch).</li>
            </ul>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Strict</b> (default)</td><td>Create the table if it is missing. If it exists it must match exactly, otherwise the job fails and reports the difference.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Evolve</b></td><td>Add new columns (also technical columns on existing tables) and widen narrow text/binary columns. Real type changes still fail.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>A pre-existing table without the technical columns is never "identical"; use Evolve or a new table name.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="storage">
            <h2>Target storage: rowstore or columnstore</h2>
            <p><b>Storage</b> in section <i>2 · Target</i> decides how a new target table is built:</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Storage</th><th>Table</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Rowstore</b> (default)</td><td>clustered index on <code>_LoadedAt, _LoadId</code> plus two nonclustered indexes, compression from <code>Collector:TargetTableCompression</code> (PAGE by default)</td>
                        <td>small and medium tables, tables queried row by row</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Columnstore</b></td><td>one clustered columnstore index, no other indexes</td>
                        <td>large tables that are read in bulk (reports, exports, BigQuery/Spark feeds)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Why it is faster:</b> rows go in batches of 1,048,576 straight into compressed rowgroups; a rowstore table maintains three B-tree indexes row by row.
                    Measured on 450,000 rows: rowstore with PAGE compression 18.4 s, without compression 11.0 s, columnstore 4.3 s. Columnstore tables are also much smaller
                    and scans (exports, reports) read only the columns they need.</li>
                <li><b>Works with every load mode</b>: Append, Replace (also with rebuild – the new table is columnstore too), Incremental, Merge (the unique key index is
                    added on top of the columnstore) and daily partitioning (retention still truncates whole days).</li>
                <li><b>Limits:</b> <code>xml</code> and <code>sql_variant</code> columns are not allowed (the load fails with the column names) – choose Rowstore or convert them in a
                    query, e.g. <code>CAST(Doc AS nvarchar(max)) AS Doc</code>. Needs SQL Server 2016 SP1 or later on the target.</li>
                <li><b>Suggestion:</b> for a new job the form suggests the storage and applies it until you pick one yourself. It estimates the rows the target will hold –
                    Append: source rows × loads per day (from the schedule) × days kept (one year when nothing is deleted); Replace, Merge, Incremental: about the source rows –
                    adds 25 % growth headroom and suggests Columnstore from 1 million rows. Example: 50,000 rows loaded every night and kept 30 days = 1.5 million rows → Columnstore.
                    <b>Merge</b> gets Columnstore only from 10 million rows: it updates changed rows, and an update in a columnstore is a delete plus an insert
                    (measured about 2× slower than PAGE rowstore); reads and exports are as fast or faster, inserts faster.
                    Views, queries and tables with xml / sql_variant columns get Rowstore. The reason is shown under the field; when you chose differently, <i>Use …</i> takes the suggestion.
                    With several tables selected every table gets its own suggestion (column <i>Storage</i> of the list).</li>
                <li>The setting applies <b>when the table is created</b>. An existing table keeps its layout; to convert one, use Replace with rebuild once or ask the DBA.</li>
                <li>Small frequent loads (e.g. a few hundred rows every 15 minutes) first land in an uncompressed delta store; SQL Server compresses it in the background.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="load-modes">
            <h2>Load modes</h2>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What happens on each run</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Append</b></td><td>All selected rows are added. Older loads stay (until retention removes them).</td><td>Daily snapshots, history</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Replace</b></td><td>All rows are loaded; after success the job's older loads are deleted.</td><td>Small reference tables</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Incremental</b></td><td>Only rows with <code>watermark column &gt; last value</code> are read. The highest value read is stored after success.</td><td>Large tables with ModifiedAt / rowversion</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Merge</b></td><td>Upsert by <a href="/help#merge">key columns</a>: changed rows are updated, new keys inserted, optionally vanished keys deleted. The table holds one row per key (the current state).
                        With <a href="/help#change-tracking">change tracking</a> only the changed keys are read, deletes included.</td><td>Master data, orders with status changes, a current-state copy for exports</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <h3>Replace: delete previous loads or rebuild and swap</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Delete previous loads</b> (default): the new load is added, then the job's older loads are deleted in batches. Grants and indexes on the table stay.</li>
                <li><b>Rebuild the table and swap it in</b>: every run loads a new table <code>&lt;table&gt;__sdc_new</code> created from the <i>current</i> source schema
                    (minimally logged with <code>TABLOCK</code>), then swaps it in with two renames and drops the old table in one short transaction.
                    Readers see either the old or the complete new table; if the load fails, the old table stays untouched. Column changes in the source are absorbed
                    (the schema policy does not apply). Good for large Replace tables, where deleting the previous load is slow and log-heavy.</li>
                <li>Rebuild only replaces tables created by the application (with <code>_LoadId</code>), only if no other job writes to the same table, and not with daily partitioning.
                    Grants on the table itself and indexes added by hand are lost on every run: grant permissions on the schema
                    (<code>GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA::erp TO …</code>) or via <code>db_datareader</code>. A view created <code>WITH SCHEMABINDING</code> on the table blocks the swap.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 id="merge">Merge (upsert)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Choose the <b>key columns</b> that identify one row (the primary key of a source table is suggested). Each run bulk-loads the source rows into a temporary table,
                    checks them (no empty key, no key twice) and then applies them to the target in <b>one transaction</b>: rows whose values changed are updated, new keys are inserted.
                    Unchanged rows are not touched, so <code>_LoadId</code> only moves for rows that really changed and incremental exports send exactly the changes.</li>
                <li><b>Full read, watermark or change tracking:</b> without a watermark column every run reads the whole source; with one (e.g. <code>ModifiedAt</code>, rowversion) only changed rows are read;
                    with <a href="/help#change-tracking">change tracking</a> only the changed keys, deletes included.</li>
                <li><b>Delete rows whose key the source no longer returns</b> (optional, full read only): such rows are deleted from the target. A run that reads no rows at all never deletes,
                    so a broken source cannot empty the table. Deleted rows do not appear in exports.</li>
                <li>The target table gets a unique index <code>UX_&lt;schema&gt;_&lt;table&gt;_Key</code> on the key on the first run (re-created when the key changes). A table that already contains a key twice
                    (e.g. from earlier Append loads) is refused with a clear message. Key columns of a <code>(max)</code> or xml type cannot be indexed: cast them in the query.</li>
                <li>Only this job may write to the target table; daily partitioning and retention do not apply (the table is the current state of the source).</li>
                <li>A failed run changes nothing (the transaction is rolled back) and needs no clean-up. The Run history shows <code>merge: +inserted ~updated -deleted</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 id="change-tracking">Change Tracking (Merge)</h3>
            <p>With <b>Read only the changes recorded by SQL Server Change Tracking</b> a Merge job of a table reads only the keys changed since its last run –
               no watermark column is needed, and deletes are seen too.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Prerequisite (DBA, once):</b> <code>ALTER DATABASE [ERP] SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 7 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);</code>, per table
                    <code>ALTER TABLE dbo.Orders ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;</code> and <code>GRANT VIEW CHANGE TRACKING ON dbo.Orders TO [service account];</code>.
                    The table needs a primary key. The form shows whether tracking is on and how long changes are kept.</li>
                <li><b>Keys</b> are always the table's primary key.</li>
                <li><b>Each run</b> first notes the current change tracking version, then reads the rows of the changed keys (with the job's columns and filter) and upserts
                    them, and deletes the keys that no longer exist – or no longer pass the filter – all in one transaction. After success the noted version is stored
                    (<i>Change tracking version</i> on the job page). A change made while the run reads is simply read again by the next run.</li>
                <li><b>Full read:</b> the first run, a run after <i>reset</i>, and a run whose stored version is older than the change retention (the job paused longer than
                    the retention) read the whole table and delete the keys that are gone. Run the job more often than the retention.</li>
                <li>Run history: rows read = changed rows; <code>merge: +inserted ~updated -deleted</code>. A row updated without a real change is not rewritten.</li>
                <li>With <i>Read a consistent snapshot</i> the version and the changes are read at one point in time. Key columns cannot be masked while collecting.
                    <a href="/help#parallel-reads">Parallel readers</a> are used for the full reads.</li>
                <li>Load on the source: change tracking records only the changed keys in an internal table (lighter than CDC); a run reads only the changed rows.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Watermark</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Supported column types: datetime, datetime2, smalldatetime, date, datetimeoffset, int, bigint, smallint, tinyint, decimal, rowversion.</li>
                <li>The value is passed with the column's real SQL type (no precision loss with datetime, index seeks are kept).</li>
                <li>Query sources must use the parameter themselves: <code>WHERE (@@WatermarkFrom IS NULL OR o.ModifiedAt &gt; @@WatermarkFrom)</code>. It is NULL on the first run.</li>
                <li><i>Reset</i> on the job page makes the next run read everything again.</li>
                <li>Rows committed late with an older watermark value are not picked up; use rowversion where possible.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="partitioning">
            <h2>Daily partitioning</h2>
            <p>If enabled when the target table is <b>created</b>, the table is partitioned by day on <code>_LoadedAt</code> (UTC).
               Partitions for the next 7 days are created automatically. Retention then removes whole days with <code>TRUNCATE … WITH (PARTITIONS)</code>
               instead of deleting rows one by one – instant, with almost no log. It has no effect on tables that already exist.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="retention">
            <h2>Retention</h2>
            <p>The retention job runs nightly (default 01:30, set on <i>Settings</i>) and removes rows older than <i>Retention (days)</i>, based on <code>_LoadedAt</code>.
               Empty = keep forever (if the policy allows it). <b>Replace</b> jobs have no retention: they keep only the latest successful load,
               so the retention policy does not apply to them (a paused Replace job keeps its last load). If several jobs write to the same table, each job's rows follow that job's retention;
               whole daily partitions are truncated only when every job writing to the table has a retention, so rows of a "keep forever" job are never lost.</p>
            <p>The organization-wide <a href="/help#settings">retention policy</a> (default, maximum, keep forever, who may change it) is enforced when a job is saved
               and again by the retention job: a longer retention is capped at the maximum, a missing one gets the default when keep forever is not allowed.
               Only tables written by the application (with <code>_LoadId</code>) are ever cleaned up.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="export">
            <h2>Exports</h2>
            <p>Export jobs write CSV or Parquet files and upload them to the job's <a href="/help#destinations">destination</a> (multipart/resumable upload, retries, size check). Files are written to the
               Worker's <code>WorkDirectory</code> in day folders first. Files are uploaded as <b>plain CSV</b> (tick <i>gzip uploaded files</i> to upload
               <code>.csv.gz</code> instead). After a successful upload a copy stays on the Worker – see <a href="/help#export-archive">Local export archive</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="export-archive">
            <h2>Local export archive</h2>
            <p>The export Worker keeps a copy of every uploaded file, so a file can be checked or sent again by hand when the receiver reports a problem.
               It is on by default and kept for <b>1 day</b>; <i>Settings → Local export archive</i> allows <b>1 to 3 days</b> (stored in the metadata database, used by every
               Worker). Copies are made only after a successful upload; CSV files are <b>always gzip-compressed</b> (<code>.csv.gz</code>), Parquet files stay as they are.</p>
            <p>Folders: <code>&lt;WorkDirectory&gt;\2026-11-26\&lt;job&gt;\</code> (single table or query) and <code>&lt;WorkDirectory&gt;\2026-11-26\&lt;job&gt;\&lt;schema.table&gt;\</code>
               (all collected tables); the day is the job's time zone.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Full</b> and <b>Query</b> exports send the whole result every time: when the job runs again on the same day (e.g. after a problem), its new files
                    <b>replace</b> that day's copy – the archive holds what was sent last.</li>
                <li><b>Incremental</b> and <b>All collected tables</b> exports send only new loads: a second run on the same day <b>adds</b> its files
                    (<code>run&lt;id&gt;_…</code>) next to the earlier ones, so nothing that was sent is lost. A failed part of a run is not archived and is sent again by the next run.</li>
                <li>Retention: the newest 1–3 day folders that hold files are kept; older ones are removed at the end of each export run. An earlier day stays
                    until a newer day has files, so at least one day of exports is always there.</li>
                <li>Disk: plan for one to three days of the compressed exports (see <code>deploy/sizing.md</code>). Only where the disk cannot hold even one day,
                    <code>Collector:KeepLocalFiles = false</code> turns the copy off for that Worker.</li>
                <li>To overwrite the uploaded objects too when a full export runs again on the same day, leave <code>{Time}</code> out of the object name template.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="destinations">
            <h2>Export destinations (GCS, S3, Azure Blob)</h2>
            <p>A <b>destination</b> is where export files go: one bucket (GCS, S3) or container (Azure) plus how to sign in. Admins manage them on
               <b>Administration → Destinations</b>; every export job picks one. Changing a destination (for example a new key or another bucket)
               applies to all jobs that use it at their next run. A destination used by jobs cannot be deleted – deactivate it instead; a run that
               finds its destination inactive fails with "is inactive" instead of writing somewhere unexpected.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Fields</th><th>Sign-in</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Google Cloud Storage</b></td><td>Bucket</td>
                        <td><i>Credential of the Settings page</i>: the key on Settings → Google Cloud Storage credential (or <code>Gcs:CredentialsPath</code> / Application Default Credentials, see
                            <a href="/help#gcs">GCS settings</a>). <i>Own credential file (WIF configuration or key)</i>: a Workload Identity Federation configuration or a service account key only for this destination.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Amazon S3 / S3-compatible</b></td><td>Bucket, region; for MinIO, Wasabi, Ceph, Cloudflare R2 etc. also the <b>endpoint</b> URL and usually <i>path-style</i></td>
                        <td><i>Access key and secret</i>: access key id + secret access key. <i>Server's AWS identity</i>: the AWS credential chain of the Worker (environment, profile, EC2/ECS role).
                            The IAM user needs <code>s3:PutObject</code> on the bucket (and <code>s3:GetObject</code> for the size check).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Azure Blob Storage</b></td><td>Container, account URL (<code>https://&lt;account&gt;.blob.core.windows.net</code>)</td>
                        <td><i>Connection string</i> (the account URL is then taken from it). <i>SAS token</i> with create/write permission. <i>Account key</i>: storage account key.
                            <i>Managed Identity (no key)</i>: <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> (managed identity, environment, Azure CLI) – the identity needs <i>Storage Blob Data Contributor</i>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>Secrets (keys, connection strings, SAS tokens) are stored <b>AES-256 encrypted</b> in the metadata database with <code>Secrets:Key</code> and are never shown
               again – leave the field empty to keep the stored value. The bucket or container must exist; the application does not create it.
               <i>Test</i> writes a small object <code>_sdc_connection_test/&lt;machine&gt;.txt</code> from the Web server; the export Worker needs the same outbound
               HTTPS access (<i>Check permissions</i> on the Servers page tests the TCP connection to each destination in use from every Worker).</p>
            <p>The object name template and the manifest work the same for every provider. Files appear as <code>gs://bucket/…</code>,
               <code>s3://bucket/…</code> or <code>https://account.blob.core.windows.net/container/…</code> in the run history.
               After an upgrade, existing GCS jobs get a destination named <code>gcs-&lt;bucket&gt;</code> automatically and keep exporting unchanged.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="export-modes">
            <h2>Export modes</h2>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What is exported</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>All collected tables</b></td><td>Every target table of the extract jobs in the chosen database (optionally one schema), <b>one CSV set per table</b>,
                        containing only the loads since the previous export of that table. Scheduled nightly, this is exactly "what was collected today".
                        New tables are picked up automatically. Each table is tracked separately: if one table fails, the others are still exported and
                        a retry only re-does what is missing (status <i>PartiallyFailed</i>).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Single table, incremental</b></td><td>Loads of one table since the last export.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Single table, full</b></td><td>The whole table every time.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Custom query</b></td><td>Any SQL; parameters <code>@@RunDate</code> (date in the job's time zone) and <code>@@RunAtUtc</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>A load that is still running is never exported half-done; it is picked up by the next run. <i>Reset export state</i> exports everything again.</p>
            <p><b>Read-only by design:</b> every export read and the export <i>Preview</i> switch to the read-only database user
               <code>Collector:ExportReadAsUser</code> (default <code>sdc_export_reader</code>) with <code>EXECUTE AS USER … WITH COOKIE</code>.
               Custom queries and column lists therefore cannot change data (<code>INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DROP</code> fail with "permission denied") and cannot
               <code>REVERT</code> to the service account. The user must exist in every database exports read from (DBA script: <code>sql/01_permissions_template.sql</code>);
               queries cannot reach other databases (three-part names) in this security context. <i>Check permissions</i> on the Servers page verifies the user.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="gcs">
            <h2>GCS settings and object names</h2>
            <p>No Google tools are needed on the servers (the client library is part of the application); the export Worker needs outbound HTTPS to
               <code>storage.googleapis.com</code> and <code>oauth2.googleapis.com</code> (with Workload Identity Federation also <code>sts.googleapis.com</code> and
               <code>iamcredentials.googleapis.com</code>). The identity needs <i>Storage Object Creator</i> (<code>roles/storage.objectCreator</code>) on the bucket –
               an API key cannot write to GCS. The bucket must exist.</p>
            <p>Credential, in this order: <code>Gcs:CredentialsPath</code> (a credential file in appsettings) → the <b>credential uploaded on Settings → Google Cloud Storage
               credential</b> (stored AES-256 encrypted in the metadata database with <code>Secrets:Key</code>, so every Worker uses it; only the identity is shown)
               → Application Default Credentials (<code>GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS</code> of the service). A credential file is one of:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Workload Identity Federation configuration</b> (<code>"type": "external_account"</code>, keyless – recommended where service account keys are not allowed):
                    the Worker reads a token of your identity provider (OIDC or SAML, e.g. AD FS or Entra ID), Google's STS exchanges it for a short-lived token and, with
                    <code>service_account_impersonation_url</code>, acts as a service account. Create it with
                    <code>gcloud iam workload-identity-pools create-cred-config projects/&lt;number&gt;/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/&lt;pool&gt;/providers/&lt;provider&gt;
                    --service-account=&lt;sa&gt;@@&lt;project&gt;.iam.gserviceaccount.com --credential-source-file=D:\SqlHarbor\token\oidc.jwt --output-file=wif.json</code>.
                    Supported token sources: a <b>file</b> (a scheduled task of the server refreshes the token before it expires), a <b>URL</b> (e.g. the Azure VM metadata
                    endpoint) and <b>AWS</b>. <i>Executable</i>- and <i>X.509 certificate</i>-sourced configurations are refused: Google's .NET library does not support them.
                    On upload the file is checked: <code>token_url</code> and <code>service_account_impersonation_url</code> must be <code>https://….googleapis.com</code>, so the
                    external token can only be sent to Google. The file holds no secret; the token file must be readable by the service account of every export Worker.</li>
                <li><b>Service account key</b> (<code>"type": "service_account"</code>): a long-lived private key – use it only where keys are allowed.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
               <i>Test connection</i> gets an access token and can write a small object <code>_sdc_connection_test/&lt;machine&gt;.txt</code> to a bucket.
               This chain is used by GCS <a href="/help#destinations">destinations</a> with sign-in <i>Credential of the Settings page</i>; a destination with its own key ignores it.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white mono">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Token</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>{Name}</td><td>job name</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>{Database} {Schema} {Table}</td><td>source of the file</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>{Date} / {DateCompact} / {Time}</td><td>2026-11-21 / 20261121 / 010000 (job time zone)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>{Part}</td><td>0001, 0002 … (file part)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>Default for all tables: <code>exports/{Database}/{Schema}.{Table}/dt={Date}/{Table}_{DateCompact}_{Time}_{Part}.csv</code>.
               The <code>dt=</code> folder works as a Hive/BigQuery partition. A run manifest is written to <code>…/_manifests/</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="csv-format">
            <h2>File formats: CSV and Parquet</h2>
            <p>Each export job writes <b>CSV</b> or <b>Parquet</b> files (section <i>3 · File format</i>). Masking rules and the export guard apply to both.</p>
            <h3>CSV</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>RFC 4180: fields containing the delimiter, quotes or line breaks are quoted; quotes are doubled.</li>
                <li>NULL is written as the <i>NULL value</i> (default empty); an empty string is written as <code>""</code> so the two stay distinguishable.</li>
                <li>Dates: <code>yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.fffffff</code> (trailing zeros removed), datetimeoffset with offset, bit as 1/0, binary as 0x… hex.</li>
                <li>decimal(38,x) values are written exactly. Text is UTF-8 (optional BOM for Excel). Uploaded files are plain CSV by default; the local archive is gzip.</li>
                <li><i>Max rows per file</i> splits large tables into parts; every part has a header row.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Parquet</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Columnar, typed and compressed inside the file (<b>Snappy</b> by default, <b>Zstd</b> for smaller files, Gzip or none). BigQuery (<code>bq load --source_format=PARQUET</code>), Spark, Databricks and pandas read it directly – no delimiter, quoting or NULL conventions to agree on.</li>
                <li>Types: bit → boolean; tinyint/smallint/int → int32; bigint → int64; real/float → float/double; decimal and money → DECIMAL with the column's precision and scale, exact (up to 38 digits);
                    date → DATE; datetime/datetime2 → TIMESTAMP in microseconds (the 7th digit of datetime2 is dropped); datetimeoffset → TIMESTAMP in UTC; binary/rowversion → bytes;
                    time, uniqueidentifier, xml and text types → string; masked columns → string.</li>
                <li>Rows are written in row groups of <code>Collector:ParquetRowGroupSize</code> rows (100,000; memory per export ≈ row group × row width). <i>Max rows per file</i> splits the output into several files.</li>
                <li>Object names end in <code>.parquet</code> (a <code>.csv</code> ending of the name template is replaced). The local archive keeps the Parquet files as they are (already compressed).</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="cron">
            <h2>Scheduling (cron)</h2>
            <p>Five fields: <code>minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week</code>, evaluated in the job's time zone. The form shows the next 5 runs.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white mono">
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>0 2 * * *</td><td>every night at 02:00</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>*/15 * * * *</td><td>every 15 minutes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>0 7 * * 1-5</td><td>weekdays at 07:00</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>30 0 1 * *</td><td>1st day of every month at 00:30</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>A job never runs twice at the same time: if the previous run is still busy, the new trigger is skipped.
               Instead of guessing times, an export can also run <a href="/help#dependencies">after its extracts</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="dependencies">
            <h2>Job dependencies (run after other jobs)</h2>
            <p>In section <i>4 · Trigger</i> of an extract or export job choose <b>After other jobs</b> and tick the jobs it waits for (extracts and exports can be mixed).
               The job then has no cron schedule: it starts automatically as soon as <b>every</b> selected job has succeeded <b>since this job was last started by them</b>.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Typical use: a nightly export that runs after all extracts of the database (<code>extract A, extract B → export</code>). Chains are possible (A → B → C).</li>
                <li>Success means <i>Succeeded</i>; for an upstream export also <i>Skipped</i> (nothing new to export). A failed or partially failed upstream keeps the dependent waiting,
                    until that upstream succeeds on a later run (retry or next schedule).</li>
                <li>Each upstream success is used once: if A runs every hour and B once a night, the dependent runs once a night (when both have a newer success).</li>
                <li>A dependency added today does not fire on yesterday's successes: only runs that finish after the dependency was saved count.</li>
                <li>Manual <i>Run now</i> of an upstream counts like a scheduled run. <i>Run now</i> of the dependent itself is always possible and does not change its waiting state.</li>
                <li>The check runs right after every run (in the Worker that executed it) and again in the periodic <i>Sync job definitions</i> job, so nothing is lost if a Worker stops.
                    If the dependent is still running, it is started again after its run ends.</li>
                <li>Loops (A after B, B after A) cannot be saved. An inactive upstream never succeeds, so its dependents wait. Deleting a job removes its dependencies.</li>
                <li>Lists and the Status page show such jobs as <span class="badge bg-light text-dark border">after</span> <i>job names</i>; the run's <i>Triggered by</i> is <code>after A, B</code>.
                    <i>Missed run</i> alerts do not apply to them (they have no schedule); failure alerts do.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="quality">
            <h2>Data quality checks</h2>
            <p>Section <i>5 · Data quality</i> of an extract job holds checks that run right after every successful load, in the Worker that loaded it.
               Results are shown on the job, on the <a href="/quality">Data quality</a> page and as a <i>quality</i> badge in the Run history.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Check</th><th>Passes when</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Minimum rows</b></td><td>the load read at least N rows (catches empty or truncated loads).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Row count change</b></td><td>the rows read are within ±N % of the average of the last 10 successful loads (needs 3 earlier loads).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Row count matches the source</b></td><td>a second count of the source table (same filter) matches the rows written by this load – for Merge, all rows of the target –
                        within the tolerance. Only for table/view sources, not for Incremental jobs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>NULL share</b></td><td>the column has at most N % NULL values, in this load or in all rows of the job.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Duplicate keys</b></td><td>at most N key values (one or more columns) occur more than once, in this load or in all rows.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Custom SQL</b></td><td>the SQL returns one number that satisfies the comparison, e.g. <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM {Table} WHERE _LoadId = @@LoadId AND Amount &lt; 0</code> must be <code>= 0</code>.
                        <code>{Table}</code> is the target table; <code>@@LoadId</code> and <code>@@ExtractJobId</code> are parameters. It runs as the read-only export reader, so a check can never change data.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Severity:</b> a broken <i>Warning</i> check marks the load's quality as Warning; a broken <i>Error</i> check (or a check that cannot run) marks it as Failed.</li>
                <li>A failed quality check never undoes the load. But jobs that <a href="/help#dependencies">run after</a> this job wait until the job's <b>latest</b> load passes (an earlier good load does not count: the table now holds the failed load's data), and the load does not count as "good" for freshness.</li>
                <li><b>Alerts:</b> <i>QualityFailed</i> and <i>QualityWarning</i> list the broken checks; they go to the job's Notify addresses and to channels that subscribe to them
                    (new channels do by default for QualityFailed; tick them on existing channels).</li>
                <li><b>Freshness:</b> "Alert when there is no good load for N hours" sends one <i>Stale</i> alert per stale period – useful for jobs triggered by other jobs, which have no schedule to miss.</li>
                <li>Results are kept as long as the run history (Settings).</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="consistency">
            <h2>Integrity and consistent reads</h2>
            <p>"What is in the source arrives in the CSV" is checked in two places that do not depend on the source standing still:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Every extract</b> (always on): the rows read from the source are counted while streaming and compared with the rows that arrived in the target
                    (this load, the rebuild table or the merge staging table). A difference fails the run and the load is rolled back.</li>
                <li><b>Every export</b> (always on): after writing, the CSV or Parquet files are read again and their rows counted (CSV records RFC 4180-aware, so line breaks
                    inside quoted values do not count twice) and compared with the rows read from the DWH; nothing is uploaded on a difference. The upload then verifies each object's size.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Consistent snapshot</h3>
            <p>A busy source changes while it is being read. <b>Read a consistent snapshot</b> (job section <i>1 · Source</i>) runs the read inside a SNAPSHOT isolation transaction:
               the job copies exactly the rows that existed at one point in time – none skipped, none twice, no uncommitted data – without blocking the source.
               <code>WITH (NOLOCK)</code> is the opposite: on a busy table it can skip rows or read them twice (page splits) and it reads uncommitted data; it is ignored when the snapshot option is on.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The DBA enables it once per source database: <code>ALTER DATABASE [ERP] SET ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION ON;</code> (row versions in tempdb while transactions run).
                    The job form shows whether it is allowed; a job that needs it but cannot use it fails with that command in the message.</li>
                <li>Readable Availability Group secondaries always read with row versioning.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Source count check on a changing source</h3>
            <p>The <a href="/help#quality">Row count matches the source</a> check counts the source again after the load. New rows written in between would make the counts differ.
               Give it a <b>cutoff column</b> – an increasing ID (identity) or an insert time – and only source rows up to the highest value that was loaded are counted.
               Rows deleted in the source after the read still show as a difference; use a small tolerance for such tables, or rely on the integrity checks above.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="transfer">
            <h2>Import and export jobs</h2>
            <p>On <a href="/transfer">Jobs → Import / export</a> job definitions are saved as a JSON file and loaded into another installation – typically from test to production,
               but also as a backup or to keep the definitions in version control. Viewers can download; Editors and Admins can import.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>What the file holds:</b> all settings of the selected extract and export jobs, the quality checks of extract jobs and the
                    <a href="/help#dependencies">dependencies</a>. <i>Also include the jobs they run after</i> adds the upstream jobs automatically.
                    Servers, destinations and upstream jobs are referenced by <b>name</b> (ids differ between installations). Keys, passwords, watermarks, run history and
                    collected data are <b>never</b> included.</li>
                <li><b>Import is checked first:</b> after choosing the file the page shows one row per job – <i>create</i>, <i>overwrite</i>, <i>unchanged</i> or <i>error</i> – and
                    changes nothing until you click <b>Import</b>. The button stays disabled while any row has an error; an error-free plan is saved completely.</li>
                <li><b>Servers and destinations</b> with the same name are used automatically. Names that do not exist here are listed with a dropdown: map e.g. <code>TEST-ERP</code> to <code>PROD-ERP</code>.</li>
                <li><b>Jobs that already exist</b> (same name) are left unchanged by default. <i>Overwrite with the file</i> replaces their settings, quality checks and dependencies;
                    an existing job keeps its active/inactive state and its watermark, so a running production job is not switched off or reloaded.</li>
                <li><b>New jobs are created inactive</b> by default, so nothing runs before you have looked at them: open the job and tick <i>Active</i>.</li>
                <li>The same checks as on <i>Save</i> apply: your server and database permissions, the retention policy, Merge / rebuild target tables,
                    cron expressions, and dependencies (an upstream must be in the file or exist here; no loops). Imports and downloads are written to the Audit log.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><b>Command line</b> (e.g. in a deployment pipeline, with the configuration of the installation):</p>
            <pre class="mono small bg-light p-2 border rounded">SqlDataCollector.exe jobs-export jobs.json [--extract 1,2] [--export 3] [--no-upstreams]
SqlDataCollector.exe jobs-import jobs.json --dry-run --map-server TEST-ERP=PROD-ERP --map-destination test-bucket=prod-bucket
SqlDataCollector.exe jobs-import jobs.json [--update-existing] [--keep-active] [--map-server FILE=HERE]... [--map-destination FILE=HERE]...</pre>
            <p class="small">Without job ids <code>jobs-export</code> writes all jobs. <code>jobs-import</code> prints the plan, returns exit code 1 and changes nothing when there is an error;
               <code>--dry-run</code> only prints the plan. The command line runs with the service account's rights (like an Admin); the Workers' sync job registers the schedules of active jobs.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="servers">
            <h2>Servers and service accounts</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>No SQL passwords are stored. Connections use the Windows account the service runs as (a gMSA is recommended).</li>
                <li>Worker account: <b>read</b> on sources (never write – Editors can write SQL), <b>write + DDL</b> on the target database
                    (<code>db_datareader, db_datawriter, db_ddladmin</code>, plus <code>ALTER ANY DATASPACE</code> for partitioning).</li>
                <li>Web account: read on sources/target (lists, preview, check target), read/write on the metadata database.</li>
                <li>Export reader: a read-only database user without login (<code>sdc_export_reader</code>) in every database exports read from; both accounts need
                    <code>IMPERSONATE</code> on it. Export SQL runs as this user, never with the Worker's write/DDL rights. Create it when the target database is set up.</li>
                <li>Both: <code>SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</code> on the Hangfire schema (<code>SqlDataCollector hangfire-install-script</code> generates the DBA script).</li>
                <li><i>ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly</i> reads sources from an Availability Group secondary; writes and incremental exports always use the primary.</li>
                <li>The <i>Servers</i> page has three sections: <b>System databases</b> (metadata and Hangfire, from configuration), <b>Target servers</b>
                    and <b>Source servers</b>. A server is used as source and/or target (at least one is required); one marked as both appears in both sections.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="servers-system">
            <h2>System databases (metadata and Hangfire)</h2>
            <p>The metadata database (<code>ConnectionStrings:Metadata</code>) and the Hangfire storage (<code>ConnectionStrings:Hangfire</code>; empty = embedded in the
               metadata database) are shown read-only. The application needs them to start, so they are set in the appsettings file of each process
               (samples in <code>deploy/</code>) followed by a service restart. The page shows the Web process's configuration; Workers have their own file.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Embedded</b>: Hangfire tables in the metadata database, schema <code>hangfire_collector</code>, <code>HangfirePrepareSchema=true</code>.</li>
                <li><b>Shared Hangfire database</b>: the organization's central Hangfire database; our own schema installed by the DBA, <code>HangfirePrepareSchema=false</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="settings">
            <h2>Settings: retention and cleanup</h2>
            <p>Admins set organization-wide policies on <i>Settings</i>. They are stored in the metadata database (<code>etl.SystemSetting</code>), apply to every process
               without a restart and every change is written to the audit log. Values not saved yet come from appsettings (marked <i>from configuration</i>).</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Default retention</b>: pre-filled in new extract jobs. <b>Maximum retention</b>: enforced on save; existing jobs with a longer retention are capped by the retention job.</li>
                <li><b>Allow keep forever</b>: if off, jobs need a retention; old jobs without one get the default (or the maximum) at retention time.</li>
                <li><b>Editors may change the retention</b>: if off, only Admins change it; Editors' new jobs get the default. Admins also follow the maximum and keep-forever rules.</li>
                <li><b>Retention schedule</b> (cron, in <code>Collector:SystemTimeZone</code>), <b>run history</b>, <b>audit log</b> and <b>permission check</b> retention in days.</li>
                <li><b>Orphaned collector tables</b>: lists tables with <code>_LoadId</code>/<code>_ExtractJobId</code> in the target databases used by jobs that no extract job writes to
                    (e.g. after a job was deleted). Retention never touches them and nothing is dropped automatically.</li>
                <li>Not managed by the application: exported objects in GCS, S3 or Azure (use a bucket lifecycle rule or lifecycle management policy), and Hangfire's own job history. <i>Local export archive</i> sets how long export Workers keep their copies (1–3 days, see
                    <a href="/help#export-archive">Local export archive</a>). Connections, roles/queues, folders and security settings (e.g. <code>ExportReadAsUser</code>) stay in appsettings.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="https">
            <h2>HTTPS certificate</h2>
            <p>With <code>Https:Port</code> set (the installer uses 443) the application serves HTTPS itself (Kestrel, no IIS). Without a certificate it creates a
               <b>self-signed</b> one for <code>Https:HostName</code>, the machine name and <code>localhost</code> (2 years) – browsers warn until you replace it.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The import below appears only while HTTPS is on. With <code>Https:Port</code> = 0 (plain HTTP, e.g. a local test) the card only explains how to switch it on:
                    set the port in <code>appsettings.json</code> and restart the service.</li>
                <li><i>Settings → HTTPS certificate</i> (Admin): import a <b>PKCS#12</b> file (<code>.pfx</code>/<code>.p12</code> with private key and its password) or pick a
                    certificate of the Windows store <code>LocalMachine\My</code> (the service account needs read access to its private key: certlm.msc → Manage Private Keys).</li>
                <li>The new certificate applies to new connections immediately – no restart. An imported file is stored DPAPI-protected in <code>Https:CertificateDirectory</code>
                    (default <code>%ProgramData%\SqlDataCollector\certs</code>), never in the database.</li>
                <li>The certificate must be valid for server authentication and not expired. 30 days before expiry the Status page warns and the <i>CertExpiring</i> alert is sent daily.</li>
                <li>Windows authentication needs HTTP/1.1; the HTTPS endpoint is HTTP/1.1 only. For Kerberos register <code>setspn -S HTTP/&lt;host&gt; &lt;service account&gt;</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="installation">
            <h2>Installation (SqlHarbor-Setup.exe)</h2>
            <p>One self-contained file: application, .NET runtime and wizard – nothing else has to be installed on the server (SQL Server is separate).
               The server must be joined to the AD domain; the service always runs as a domain account (or gMSA).</p>
            <ol>
                <li><b>Mode</b>: Embedded (one server, Hangfire in the metadata database) or Central (the organization's Hangfire database; Web and/or Worker on this server).</li>
                <li><b>Service account</b>: verified against the domain and given "Log on as a service".</li>
                <li><b>Databases</b>: <i>create now</i> with the installing user's Windows account (databases, etl schema, permissions, export reader), or <i>later</i>:
                    the scripts are written to <code>&lt;program folder&gt;\sql</code> for the DBA. Central Hangfire: <code>03_hangfire_central.sql</code> is always for the DBA.
                    Until the metadata database is ready the application shows a <i>setup required</i> page and restarts itself as soon as it is.</li>
                <li><b>Web</b>: host name and HTTPS port, self-signed certificate, firewall rule; program and data folder (certificate) and the <b>export work folder</b>
                    (<code>Collector:WorkDirectory</code>: temporary export files and the optional local archive; empty = <code>&lt;data folder&gt;\exports</code>).
                    Choose a fast local disk other than C: with room for the largest tables of one night; network shares are refused. An upgrade keeps the folder in use –
                    to move it later, change <code>Collector:WorkDirectory</code> in <code>appsettings.Production.json</code> and restart the service.</li>
                <li>The service starts; <i>Open SqlHarbor</i> opens <code>https://localhost/login</code> for the first sign-in.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Silent: <code>SqlHarbor-Setup.exe /silent /config install.json /password-env SDC_PWD [/log file] [/dryrun]</code>. Uninstall: Apps &amp; features,
               or <code>/uninstall [/purge]</code> (databases and, without /purge, the data and export folders are kept). The silent JSON sets the export folder with <code>"WorkDir"</code>. Running the setup again upgrades the binaries and keeps the configuration.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="alerts">
            <h2>Alerts (e-mail, Teams, webhook)</h2>
            <p>The Web process checks every minute for runs that ended <b>Failed</b>, <b>PartiallyFailed</b>, <b>Abandoned</b> or <b>CleanupPending</b>,
               for jobs that <b>recovered</b> (first success after a failure), for scheduled runs that <b>did not start</b> (<i>Missed</i>) and for queues
               without a live Hangfire server for 5 minutes (<i>NoServer</i>). Because it runs in the Web process, it also reports when no Worker is running.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Channels</b> (menu <i>Alerts</i>, Admin): e-mail recipients, a Microsoft Teams Workflows webhook (Adaptive Card) or a generic JSON webhook
                    (also accepted by Slack). Each channel chooses its events, extract and/or export jobs and an optional job name filter (<code>erp_*; crm_*</code>).
                    <i>Send test</i> sends a sample alert.</li>
                <li><b>Notify</b> field of a job: e-mail addresses of the job's owners; they get failures, missed runs and the recovery of that job.</li>
                <li><b>Repeat interval</b>: the same problem of the same job is sent to a destination at most once per interval (default 60 minutes);
                    repeats are recorded as <i>Suppressed</i>. A recovery is always sent. Failed deliveries are retried up to 3 times (once a minute).</li>
                <li><b>Missed</b>: a scheduled occurrence counts as missed when no run started within the grace period (default 15 minutes) and none is running.</li>
                <li>Alerting starts when the application first runs with this feature: older runs are never reported. Delivery history: <i>Recent deliveries</i>
                    on the Alerts page (kept as long as the run history).</li>
                <li>Infrastructure is configured in appsettings: <code>Alerts:Smtp:Host/Port/Security/From</code> (an internal relay is recommended; user name and
                    password only if required, never stored in the database) and <code>Alerts:PublicUrl</code> for links. Webhook URLs are secrets: they are shown and audited masked.
                    The Web server needs outbound access to the SMTP server and the webhook hosts; <i>Check permissions</i> tests the TCP connection.</li>
                <li>A completely stopped application cannot alert: monitor <code>/health</code> with your monitoring system.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="masking">
            <h2>Data protection: masking and the export guard</h2>
            <p>Admins define masking rules on <i>Administration → Data protection</i>. A rule matches columns by name (<code>*Email*;*Mail</code>, wildcards <code>*</code> and <code>?</code>,
               case-insensitive) within a scope <code>Database.Schema.Table</code> (<code>*</code> = everywhere, e.g. <code>ERP.dbo.*</code>). A rule for a specific scope wins over an "everywhere" rule.</p>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Result</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Hash</b></td><td>64 hex characters (HMAC-SHA256 with a secret key derived from <code>Secrets:Key</code>). The same value always gives the same hash, so masked columns can still be joined and counted; without the key it cannot be reversed, not even for 11-digit IDs.</td><td>National IDs, customer numbers used as keys</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Partial</b></td><td>Only the last N characters stay visible: <code>**********************1326</code>.</td><td>IBAN, card and phone numbers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Email</b></td><td><code>a***@@corp.com</code> – the domain stays for statistics.</td><td>E-mail addresses</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Redact</b> / <b>Null</b></td><td><code>***</code> / no value.</td><td>Free text, notes, addresses</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Applies to – exported files only:</b> the DWH keeps the real values; every exported file (CSV or Parquet) contains the masked values. Masked columns are listed in the export manifest.</li>
                <li><b>Applies to – already when collecting:</b> extract jobs write masked values into the DWH (matched against the source object and the target table), so nothing downstream ever sees the real value.
                    Hash, Partial, Email and Redact turn non-text columns into text columns of new target tables; an existing Strict table must be re-created. The watermark column is never masked.
                    Use Hash for Merge key columns (Partial could make two keys equal).</li>
                <li>Masking applies from the next run; already exported files and already collected rows are not changed.</li>
                <li>Free SQL in a custom-query export can rename a column (<code>SELECT Email AS X</code>) and so bypass a name-based rule: that is what the export guard is for.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Export guard</h3>
            <p>The first 500 rows of every exported dataset are checked for e-mail addresses, Turkish national IDs (TCKN, with checksum), IBANs (mod-97 check), payment card numbers (Luhn check)
               and Turkish phone numbers – only in columns no rule masks. A column is reported when at least half of its sampled values match.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Warn</b> (default): the export runs; the run gets a <i>Data protection warning</i> naming the columns (Run history, Status).</li>
                <li><b>Block</b>: the dataset is not uploaded and its local files are deleted; the run fails (AllTables exports continue with the other tables). Add a rule and run again.</li>
                <li><b>Off</b>: no check.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Personal data scan</h3>
            <p>Samples the latest 200 rows of every collected table of a target database and lists columns that look like personal data, with the rule that already masks them.
               <i>Create rule</i> pre-fills a rule for exactly that table and column with a suggested method. The scan only reads; examples are shown partly hidden.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="permission-check">
            <h2>Permission check</h2>
            <p><b>Check permissions</b> on the <i>Servers</i> page compares the service accounts' effective permissions with the minimum of
               <code>sql/01_permissions_template.sql</code>, using <code>HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME</code> only (no data is read or written).</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The Web account is checked immediately. For Worker accounts a check job is queued on every queue (<code>sdc_extract</code>, <code>sdc_export</code>,
                    <code>sdc_system</code>) a live Hangfire server listens to; the process that runs it checks its own account. Click <i>Refresh results</i> after a few seconds.
                    If several servers listen to the same queue, only one of them runs the job; every Worker also checks itself on startup and logs problems as warnings.</li>
                <li>What is checked depends on the process: metadata and Hangfire for everyone; target databases used by jobs for <code>sdc_extract</code>/<code>sdc_system</code> (load, DDL,
                    <code>ALTER ANY DATASPACE</code> when a job uses daily partitions), <code>sdc_export</code> (read) and the Web (read + <code>VIEW DEFINITION</code>);
                    source objects of the jobs for <code>sdc_extract</code> and the Web. For databases that exports read from, <code>sdc_export</code> and the Web also switch to the
                    export reader user and verify that it can read and cannot write.</li>
                <li>Statuses: <span class="badge bg-success">Ok</span>, <span class="badge bg-danger">Missing</span> (a required permission is not granted),
                    <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Excess</span> (granted but must not be: write/DDL on sources, write on targets for a Web-only process,
                    rights outside our schema in a shared Hangfire database), <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">Warning</span> (cannot be determined exactly, e.g. free SQL queries
                    without database-wide <code>SELECT</code>), <span class="badge bg-danger">Error</span> (connection failed).</li>
                <li>Excess on sources matters: Editors can run free SQL there, so the service account's permissions are the security boundary.
                    A local test environment that runs as <code>sysadmin</code> shows Excess everywhere – that is expected.</li>
                <li>The latest result per process (role, queue, host) is kept; results older than 30 days are removed.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="roles">
            <h2>Roles and permissions</h2>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Role</th><th>Can do</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><b>Admin</b></td><td>Everything: servers, permissions, all jobs, audit, full Hangfire dashboard.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Editor</b></td><td>Create, change, run and delete jobs on the servers/databases granted to them.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><b>Viewer</b></td><td>See jobs, status and run history; read-only Hangfire dashboard.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>Roles and server/database permissions are assigned to AD users or groups. A permission without a database applies to all databases on that server.
               Changes reach signed-in users within 10 minutes. The local <code>admin</code> account is for initial setup and emergencies.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="hangfire">
            <h2>Hangfire dashboard</h2>
            <p>The dashboard (menu <i>Hangfire ↗</i>) shows queues, running/failed jobs, retries and Workers. Admins can trigger, retry or delete jobs there;
               other roles see it read-only. Job definitions and schedules are edited only in this application.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="monitoring">
            <h2>Timeline, trends and pipelines</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Run timeline</b> (Status): one lane per job, one bar per run – the bar starts when the run started and is as long as it took. Green succeeded,
                    amber partially failed or a quality warning, red failed, blue running; the vertical line is now. Choose 6, 12 or 24 hours or 3 days.
                    Hover a bar for times, duration and rows; click it to open the job. <b>↳</b> marks jobs that run after other jobs. Overlapping bars show jobs that
                    compete for the same night window; a bar that keeps getting longer is a job worth a look.</li>
                <li><b>Trends</b> (Status, job table): the rows and the duration of the last 30 runs as small lines. The last point turns amber when it is less than half
                    or more than twice the usual value (the median of the earlier runs, at least five) – e.g. a table that suddenly delivers a quarter of its rows.
                    A red ring means the last run failed.</li>
                <li><b>Pipelines</b> (menu): every export and every job that runs after other jobs, with the jobs they wait for, left to right, and the destinations
                    the exports upload to. Boxes have the colour of the last run; a dashed gray box is <i>waiting</i>: it runs after other jobs and one of them has not
                    succeeded yet (the box names it). Inactive jobs are gray. Extract jobs without dependents are listed as a count below the graph.</li>
                <li>Everything is drawn by the application itself (no external scripts), so it works on servers without internet access.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="troubleshooting">
            <h2>Statuses and troubleshooting</h2>
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered bg-white">
                <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-success">Succeeded</span></td><td>Completed.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-primary">Running</span></td><td>In progress.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-danger">Failed</span></td><td>Error; Hangfire retries automatically (extract 2×, export 3×). Click the row for details.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">PartiallyFailed</span></td><td>All-tables export: some tables failed, the rest were exported.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-secondary">Skipped</span></td><td>Nothing to export, or the source table does not exist yet.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Abandoned</span></td><td>The Worker stopped during the run; partial data was cleaned up.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="badge bg-danger">CleanupPending</span></td><td>A failed load could not be removed yet; the sync job retries.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <h3>Common errors</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Strict schema policy: target … does not match</b> – use <i>Check target</i>; fix the table, rename the target or switch to Evolve.</li>
                <li><b>Duplicate / unnamed column</b> – give every computed column a unique alias in the query.</li>
                <li><b>Unsupported type geography/geometry/hierarchyid</b> – convert it in the query (<code>.ToString()</code>).</li>
                <li><b>Login failed / cannot open database</b> – the service account lacks access on that server/database.</li>
                <li><b>Jobs never start</b> – no Worker is running or it does not listen to the job's queue (see Status).</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        }
    </div>
</div>

